<h2>A death foretold in a police complaint</h2>
<p>On 25 May 2012, Vineeta Katara and her two children — a boy of about two and a girl of about four — were found dead in the family\'s flat in Mehrauli, all three deaths recorded as asphyxia from antemortem hanging. Earlier that same day, Vineeta had lodged a complaint at the police station alleging physical and mental harassment by her husband and in-laws; her brother-in-law then received a call in which she said she was ending her life. The trial court convicted the husband under Sections 498A and 306 IPC, acquitted him of dowry death under Section 304-B, and acquitted the mother-in-law entirely. Both the conviction and the acquittal were tested in the High Court — and both survived.</p>

<h2>Why the dowry death charge failed</h2>
<p>Section 304-B is not a general homicide-by-cruelty provision. Its distinctive element is the connective tissue: the cruelty or harassment soon before death must be for, or in connection with, a demand for dowry. Justice Madhu Jain held that the record showed earlier monetary demands and showed cruelty shortly before the death — but did not establish the link between the two at the relevant time. In the Court\'s words, the evidence of earlier monetary demands and the evidence of cruelty shortly before Vineeta\'s death must not be conflated. Without that link, the mandatory presumption under Section 113-B of the Evidence Act never arose, and the acquittal under Section 304-B was affirmed.</p>

<h2>Why the abetment conviction stood</h2>
<p>Section 306 asks a different question: did the accused, by cruelty or otherwise, abet the suicide? Here the building blocks were the parents\' testimony of sustained harassment, the threats held out to the deceased, and the complaint she lodged on the morning of her death — a nearly contemporaneous record of a continuing course of wilful conduct. With the suicide occurring well within seven years of the 2007 marriage and cruelty under Section 498A proved, Section 113-A of the Evidence Act permitted the court to presume abetment. The Court consciously exercised that discretion, holding that the nature of the cruelty, the threats and the same-day complaint justified drawing the presumption, and distinguished the recent line of Supreme Court decisions that caution against convicting for abetment on general matrimonial discord alone — those cases, the Court held, turned on their own gentler facts.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table class="law">
<tr><th></th><th>Section 304-B IPC (S.80 BNS)</th><th>Section 306 IPC (S.108 BNS)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Core element</td><td>Death within 7 years, cruelty soon before death linked to dowry demand</td><td>Abetment of suicide — instigation or wilful conduct driving the deceased to it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Presumption</td><td>S.113-B Evidence Act — mandatory once ingredients proved</td><td>S.113-A — discretionary, for a wife\'s suicide within 7 years with proved cruelty</td></tr>
<tr><td>Outcome here</td><td>Acquittal affirmed — dowry link not proved</td><td>Conviction affirmed — presumption drawn</td></tr>
</table></div>

<h2>For the practitioner</h2>
<p>The judgment is a careful specimen of charge-by-charge analysis, and it cuts both ways. Prosecutors cannot treat every matrimonial suicide as a dowry death: the dowry nexus must be proved for the period proximate to the death, not merely somewhere in the history of the marriage. Defence counsel, conversely, cannot assume that defeating the dowry link ends the case: the same evidence of cruelty, if wilful and persistent, can sustain Sections 498A and 306 with the aid of Section 113-A — especially where, as here, the deceased left an almost contemporaneous record of her treatment. In Delhi\'s sessions courts these charges are routinely framed in the alternative, and this decision shows exactly how a court separates them at the end of trial.</p>
